# rule.always.test-a-claim-against-the-whole-it-speaks-for

> **a claim made from one case, one part, or one word speaks for a whole. test it against that whole before it lands. one case gives a pattern with a bound, never the general rule.**

| the claim | the whole to test it against |
|---|---|
| a pattern from one case | its bound: the shape that makes it hold, and one case where it fails |
| a definition from one example | the cadence the org runs: the log, the tags, the last ten units, at the real grain |
| "this system retires" | every role it plays today (dns, cdn, aliases, jobs), each with an heir or marked kept |
| a new term | the term briefs (learner `domain-term-synonyms`) |

a gap in a sequence a peer wrote is a fence: `ask-why-then-ask-can-we-delete`. failure forms: one case for a rule · the case that would break it not sought · a rate from a small sample (say the count).
test: **which whole did I test this claim on?**
blocker: a retire claim checked against the replaced role only, and a role with no named heir · a definition shipped from one example that a read of the cadence would have broken
nitpick: a pattern from one case stated as the general rule, with no bound · a new term with no read of the vocabulary behind it · a rate stated with no count
